# Firestore cart persistence (phase 6). Copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
# The app works WITHOUT these: cart persistence falls back to localStorage and
# the Firebase SDK is never loaded. Set them to turn Firestore on.
#
# Get them from the Firebase console: Project settings -> General ->
# "Your apps" -> Web app -> SDK setup and configuration.
#
# These are NOT secrets. Only VITE_-prefixed vars reach client code and
# everything in a browser bundle is public. Firebase web config is meant to be
# public; access is controlled by Firestore security rules.

VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Required Firestore setup
# -----------------------
# 1. Create a Firestore database (production mode is fine).
# 2. Because Firebase Auth is out of scope (CLAUDE.md section 3), carts are keyed
#    by a random id in localStorage and the rules must allow unauthenticated
#    access to that one collection:
#
#      rules_version = '2';
#      service cloud.firestore {
#        match /databases/{database}/documents {
#          match /carts/{cartId} {
#            allow read, write: if true;
#          }
#        }
#      }
#
#    This is acceptable for a course project. It is NOT safe for real customer
#    data — anyone who learns a cart id can read or overwrite it, and there is
#    no rate limiting. Adding Firebase Auth is what would let the rules require
#    request.auth != null.
# 3. Add the same VITE_ vars in Vercel: Project Settings -> Environment
#    Variables (CLAUDE.md section 13).
